&gt; If we're going full utilitarian here, you'd have to ask yourself how often someone comes along who is a "mad scientist" who is not only willing to torture for medical knowledge, but is capable of it. As in skilled enough to add to medical knowledge.
&gt; 

A number of psychologists and doctors were employed to help develop "enhanced interrogation techniques" for a major government not too long ago. It's caused a [big controversy](http://www.apa.org/monitor/2015/09/cover-policy.aspx) involving the ethics bodies that should be policing this behavior rather than participating in it.

Let's use the full context:

&gt; “I’ve got to use some Tic Tacs, just in case I start kissing her,” a politician says. “You know I’m automatically attracted to beautiful — **I just start kissing them. It’s like a magnet. Just kiss. I don’t even wait.**”
&gt; 
&gt; “And when you’re a star, they let you do it,” the politician says. “You can do anything.”
&gt; 
&gt; “Whatever you want,” says another voice, apparently belonging to a political rival.
&gt; 
&gt; **“Grab them by the p---y,” the politician says. “You can do anything.”**

That's sexual assault he's describing.
